        },
        "options": {}
      },
      "notes": "location_id is ACCEPTED (2026-08-10, exec 45): a credentialed GET succeeded and returned the location's 13 opportunities, so the old UNVERIFIED worry that GHL rejects this parameter name is disproved. It is NOT proven that GHL reads it: the token is a sub-account PIT, so the location is already implied by the credential, and meta.nextPageUrl echoing location_id= shows an echo, not a parse. No control run (locationId, omitted, or wrong) was made. GET only -- this workflow issues no write to GHL. Bind a Header Auth credential holding a Private Integration Token scoped to opportunities.readonly; credentials are stripped from this export."
    },
    {
      "id": "e3b46480-7dd9-45a2-96ec-8e9ce9ea3157",
      "name": "Select Candidates",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.code",
      "typeVersion": 2,
      "position": [
        448,
        272
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "jsCode": "// Turn GHL's opportunity page into candidate delivery items.\n//\n// The sweep exists because no retry can rescue a webhook that never arrived:\n// there is nothing to retry. This is the only path that can notice an\n// Opportunity GHL created and n8n never heard about.\n//\n// LOOKBACK is deliberately much wider than the 10-minute schedule. A sweep\n// that only looked back one interval would lose anything falling in a gap\n// where n8n itself was down -- which is exactly when webhooks go missing.\nconst LOOKBACK_MINUTES = 24 * 60;\nconst MAX_CANDIDATES = 100;\n\nconst cutoff = Date.now() - LOOKBACK_MINUTES * 60 * 1000;\n\nconst page = $input.first().json ?? {};\nconst opps = Array.isArray(page.opportunities) ? page.opportunities : [];\n\nconst str = (v) => (v === undefined || v === null) ? '' : String(v).trim();\n\n// The Opportunity name is built by the GHL workflow as '<name> - <service>'.\n// The service interest itself lives on a CONTACT custom field, which this\n// sweep cannot read without a second scope it does not have and did not ask\n// for. Recovering it from the name is a derivation, not the source value, and\n// it is recorded as such -- an honest partial beats either a blank column or\n// a scope nobody justified.\nconst deriveService = (name) => {\n  const parts = str(name).split(' - ');\n  return parts.length > 1 ? parts[parts.length - 1].trim() : '';\n};\n\nconst out = [];\n\nfor (const o of opps) {\n  const createdAt = str(o.createdAt);\n  if (!createdAt || new Date(createdAt).getTime() < cutoff) continue;\n\n  const opportunityId = str(o.id);\n  if (!opportunityId) continue;\n\n  const contact = (o.contact && typeof o.contact === 'object') ? o.contact : {};\n\n  out.push({\n    json: {\n      // Identical derivation to the webhook path. If these two ever disagree\n      // the sweep would recover an event the ledger already completed and\n      // write a second row, so this line is load-bearing.\n      eventId: 'ghl:opportunity-created:' + opportunityId,\n      correlationId: 'n8n:sweep:' + $execution.id,\n      timestamp: createdAt,\n      opportunityId,\n      contactId: str(o.contactId),\n      name: str(contact.name),\n      email: str(contact.email),\n      phone: str(contact.phone),\n      business: '',\n      service: deriveService(o.name),\n      source: str(o.source),\n      stage: str(o.pipelineStageId),\n      appointment: '',\n      lastAction: 'reconciled'\n    }\n  });\n\n  if (out.length >= MAX_CANDIDATES) break;\n}\n\nreturn out;\n"
      }
    },
